  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on computing and comparing variances, which requires consideration of both outcomes and their deviations from the mean. The individual did not consider the size of the outcomes in relation to their expectations or dispersion; instead, they focused only on the probability of winning a non-zero amount. There is no overlap between the information used by the individual (probability of non-zero outcome) and the information required by the variance-based reason (outcomes, probabilities, expected values, squared deviations). Similarly, the justification—choosing B due to higher winning chance—does not align with a rule based on minimizing variance, even though both lead to selecting B. The reasoning process is fundamentally different. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used variance as a decision criterion.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
